-- IELTS 6.0: lessons, practice sets and final tests for the three chặng that came over from the former 6.0 course, which
-- had points but nothing to learn from, and an objective practice set for the opinion-essay point, whose only set was a
-- single essay too short to probe or review with.

-- Reading: skimming for structure and completing a summary.

A. In some cities almost half of all short journeys are made on foot, while in others walking has almost disappeared. Researchers have spent two decades trying to explain the difference.

B. The first factor is distance. Where homes, shops and schools are mixed together, people can reach daily needs within ten minutes, and walking becomes the obvious choice.

C. The second factor is the quality of the route. Wide pavements, trees and frequent crossings make people feel safe; long waits at traffic lights and narrow paths beside fast roads discourage them.

D. Finally, habits matter. Children who walk to school are more likely to walk as adults, so some cities now pay for supervised "walking buses" to keep the habit alive.', 'VALID');
