Mangroves are trees that grow in salty water along tropical coasts. Their thick roots slow down waves and hold the mud in place, so coasts with healthy mangroves suffer far less damage in storms. The forests are also nurseries for young fish, which shelter among the roots until they are big enough to survive in open water. In recent decades, however, large areas have been cleared for shrimp farms and hotels. Several countries now pay local communities to replant mangroves, and the young trees can protect a village within about ten years.', 'VALID');
